@page "/docs/architecture/what-each-render-mode-adds"

@* What each render mode adds: the customers page of DataAccess.razor counted in each render mode (what you
   write, how many projects, what runs while the page is open), and the order to add them in. The pieces
   themselves are in DataAccessServer.razor, DataAccessBrowser.razor and SignIn.razor; this article counts
   them and links there. *@

<ArticlePage Section="architecture" Slug="what-each-render-mode-adds">
    <Lead>
        You choose a render mode for each page, and each one costs something to build and to run. A static page is
        four pieces in one project. The same page in the browser is seven pieces in two. Count them before you make
        a page interactive.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">One page, four render modes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The page is the customer list from
            <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access">Data access by render mode</Anchor>. It asks an interface,
            <Code>ICustomerStore</Code>, for its rows and shows them in a <Code>Table</Code>. The page's markup is the
            same in every render mode. What changes is what you write around it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Render mode</th><th>What you write</th><th>Projects</th><th>While the page is open</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Static</td><td>4 pieces: the page, the interface, a store, one registration</td><td>1</td><td>Nothing runs. Each click is a request.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Interactive Server</td><td>The same 4, and one line on the page</td><td>1</td><td>A circuit on the server</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>WebAssembly</td><td>7 pieces: those 4, a client store, endpoints, a second registration</td><td>2</td><td>The .NET runtime in the browser, calling your API</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Auto</td><td>The same 7</td><td>2</td><td>A circuit on the first visit, the browser's runtime after that</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>

        <Heading Level="2">Static: four pieces</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Code>Customers.razor</Code>, the page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>ICustomerStore</Code> and its row type, <Code>CustomerRow</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>EfCustomerStore</Code>, which reads your <Code>DbContext</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>One line in <Code>Program.cs</Code> that registers the store.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            The table still sorts, filters and pages, through the URL, and a form on the page still validates and
            saves, through a form post. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-server">Data access on the server</Anchor> for the store.
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/crud"><Code>izzy add crud</Code></Anchor> writes these pieces for a model you name.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Interactive Server: one line more</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Customers.razor">@Server</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The four pieces stay as they are. The store already opens a context for each call, which a circuit needs.
            The cost is at run time: the server holds each open page in memory and stays connected to it. An app on
            several servers must send each user back to the same one, and a dropped connection needs
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/session-recovery">session recovery</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">WebAssembly and Auto: seven pieces in two projects</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser can't reach your database, so the page's data crosses an API. The page and the interface move to
            the <Code>.Client</Code> project, and three pieces are new:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Code>Customers.razor</Code>, moved to <Code>.Client</Code>, with its render mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>ICustomerStore</Code> and <Code>CustomerRow</Code>, moved to <Code>.Client</Code> so both projects see them.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>EfCustomerStore</Code>, unchanged, on the server. The page prerenders there, and an Auto page runs there on the first visit.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The server's registration, unchanged.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>New:</strong> <Code>HttpCustomerStore</Code>, in <Code>.Client</Code>, which calls your API.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>New:</strong> the endpoints it calls, which repeat the page's authorization. A user can call them without your page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>New:</strong> the client's registration, with its <Code>HttpClient</Code>.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            Auto adds nothing to this list and removes nothing: the page runs in both places, so it needs both
            stores. An app with sign-in also registers the user's state in both projects. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-browser">Data access in the browser</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/sign-in">Sign-in across render modes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Add them in this order</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Start static.</strong> IzzyBlazor's components respond in the browser on a static page: dialogs
                and menus open, tabs switch, tables sort and filter, forms validate and save. Most pages need no more.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Give one component a render mode</strong> when one part of a page needs C# between requests,
                such as a feed that updates by itself. The rest of the page stays static. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/interactive-parts">Interactive parts on static pages</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Make the page Interactive Server</strong> when most of it does. It costs one line.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Move the page to the browser</strong> when it should run without a circuit, or answer each
                click without a trip to the server. Add the three new pieces for the data that page uses, not for the
                whole app.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            Each step leaves the page's markup alone, because the page asks the interface and never the database.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what each render mode is, and what components do in each.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/create-app#choose-the-render-modes">Create an app</Anchor>: the starter's option for each render mode.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Server = """
        @page "/customers"
        @rendermode InteractiveServer
        @inject ICustomerStore Customers
        """;
}
